Why /api/health and not /api/agents:
- /api/health is a stateless 200 OK (routes/health.rs) — no DB, no Docker
- /api/agents is a real handler that calls AgentRegistry.list() and
  observe_with(docker, ...) per agent. Under load it competes with the
  same tokio runtime as streaming sessions, harness calls, reconciler
- A liveness probe should not require backend state to answer

Why two-failure threshold:
- One 5s poll missing its 3s timeout is not a real outage signal
- Two consecutive failures = ~10s of unresponsiveness, which is a
  reasonable lower bound for "actually broken"
- Kept the auto-reload-on-recover behavior unchanged — once we've
  decided the server was gone, full reload is still right because
  in-flight SSE/streams will be stale

Why 8s timeout, not e.g. 15s:
- 5s poll interval; a timeout >= 2x interval lets polls overlap, which
  could mask state. 8s gives a real handler enough headroom for one
  garbage-collection / contention spike without overlapping the next poll

Rejected alternatives:
- Move the health check to a Tauri command (rust-side fetch). Heavier;
  doesn't help users running in browser; would still hit the same axum
  endpoint. The frontend-only fix is enough.
- Disable the modal entirely in Tauri. Tauri restarts happen too; users
  still need the "server gone" signal.
